---
/**
 * The one page that has to be true.
 *
 * Every claim here is something the repo can demonstrate today — the install is
 * two files, the spec is the source of truth, undo is a table lookup, export is
 * a `pg_dump`. Nothing about the AI layer, which is Phase 1 and not shipped:
 * doc 12's technical reader checks, and a marketing page that oversells the
 * unbuilt half is the fastest way to lose them.
 */
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
---

<Page
  title="forinda-cms — a CMS you own, that an AI can drive"
  description="Self-hostable CMS built on a declarative site spec. Two files to install, every change reversible, your data exportable with one command."
>
  <h1>A CMS you own, that an AI can drive.</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Your site is a <strong>spec</strong> — content types, pages, logic, access — not a pile of
    plugins and a database nobody can read. That is what makes the whole thing editable by a
    person, by a command line, or by a model, without three implementations of the same rules.
  </p>

  <div class="ctas">
    <a class="cta" href="/docs/install/">Install it — free</a>
    <a class="cta secondary" href="/features/">See what it does</a>
  </div>

  <h2>Install it in two files</h2>

  <pre><code>mkdir my-site && cd my-site
curl -O https://forinda-cms.dev/install/compose.yaml
curl -o .env https://forinda-cms.dev/install/env.example
docker compose up -d</code></pre>

  <p>
    No migration step, no setup command. The app migrates and provisions itself on boot, so
    <code>up</code> reaches a working site rather than a 404 and an instruction — then your
    dashboard is at <code>/admin</code>. <a href="/docs/install/">The full walkthrough</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What that buys you</h2>

  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Every change is reversible</h3>
      <p>Each edit is stored with its inverse, so undo is a table lookup — whether it came from a
        form, the CLI, or something automated.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Destructive changes ask first</h3>
      <p>Removing a field that holds data is refused unless you confirm it, and the warning says
        how many rows are affected.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Your data leaves whenever</h3>
      <p>One command writes a plain <code>pg_dump</code> any Postgres can read. No export queue, no
        account, no paywall.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>SEO is structural</h3>
      <p>Server-rendered pages, clean permalinks, a sitemap that updates itself, and an automatic
        301 when a page moves.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two ways in, one set of rules</h2>

  <p>
    Non-technical owners get forms and a plain-language diff before anything lands. Developers get
    a spec directory under version control:
  </p>

  <pre><code>fcms link https://my-site.example
fcms login
fcms plan      # what would change, in words
fcms apply     # destructive changes need --yes</code></pre>

  <p>
    Both go through the same validation, the same destructive gate, and the same history. There is
    no privileged door.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it is</h2>

  <p>
    Self-hostable and doing real work: the spec and the language, the renderer, the admin and its
    visual canvas, the CLI, an MCP server, the assistant, and the install above. On top of that, a
    business that takes bookings has the whole path — search by date or distance, a multi-step
    journey, a deposit, automations that confirm and notify, and each visitor seeing their own
    bookings and nobody else's.
  </p>

  <p>
    What is <em>not</em> done, said plainly, because finding out later is worse: the M-Pesa and
    messaging providers are written against their documented APIs and have never been run against a
    live account — the product says so where you would use them, and taking payment at the counter
    works today with no account at all. Plugins have a contract but no marketplace, and installing
    one is a deploy rather than a button. There is no hosted service yet; this is software you run.
  </p>
</Page>
